4,"*NON STOP CONGO MUSIC* (Koffi Olomidé, Extra Musica, JB M ...AFRICA | 101 Last Tribes - Barambo peopleBarambu people - WikipediaEndangered Languages Project - Barambu - GuideMusic of the Democratic Republic of the Congo - WikipediaClassic African Oldies Mix – Congolese Rumba | Kasongo ...",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jo1yH_KP_Ss,"Apr 9, 2022 · *NON STOP CONGO MUSIC* (Koffi Olomidé, Extra Musica, JB M'piana, Tabu Ley, Awilo Longomba, Madilu) The Barambu (or Barambo) are an ethnic group who live in the northeast of the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The Abarambo (Barambo), with whom are tentatively included the Amadi (with almost no information) and the Duga, are reported to resemble the Mangbetu in culture. The Barambu (or Barambo) are an ethnic group who live in the northeast of the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The Joshua Project as of 2020 gave the population as 61,000 located in the Poko territory of Bas-Uélé province, between the Bomokandi and Uélé rivers. [aka Barambo, Amiangba, Amiangbwa] Classification: Niger-Congo · vulnerable DescriptionResourcesActivityRevitalizationBibliographySuggest a ChangeSubscribe In both the former French Congo (now the Republic of the Congo) and Belgian Congo (now Democratic Republic of the Congo), the popular partnered dance music was initially known as maringa. Golden‑age rumba / rhumba / Lingala classics in one 3‑hour journey! Relive the timeless sebene guitars and velvet vocals of Franco, Tabu Ley, Mbilia Bel, Papa Wemba, Super Mazembe and more—perfect for Sunday chill, long drives, or a nostalgia dance‑floor. Stream, share, and download the audio at supremacysounds.com. 00:00 1. Who are the Barambu? The Barambu (or Barambo) are an ethnic group who live in the northeast of the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The Joshua Project as of 2020 gave the population as 61,000 located in the Poko territory of Bas-Uélé province, between the Bomokandi and Uélé rivers. Who are the Barambu & abarambo? The Barambu (or Barambo) are an ethnic group who live in the northeast of the Democratic Republic of the Congo. The Abarambo (Barambo), with whom are tentatively included the Amadi (with almost no information) and the Duga, are reported to resemble the Mangbetu in culture. When did Congo rumba start? In 1934, Jean Réal, a French director of entertainment from Martinique, coined the term ""Congo Rumba"" when he founded a band of the same name in Brazzaville in 1938. Soon after, several institutions that played a key role in the growth of Congolese popular music were founded. Is Barambu a 'threatened' language? According to Glottolog, the Barambu language as of 2017 had AES status ""threatened"" and may be classified as: ^ a b Barambu, Amiangba ...",Barambu,Niger-Congo
